'''''The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny: Tethan Battle Adventure''''' is a "[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny|Decide Your Destiny]]" young-reader book. The events of the book take place concurrently with those of the [[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (film)|''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film]]. Though it was the third in the series and was released on [[January 7]], [[2010]], [[StarWars.com]] originally announced it as the second with incorrect release dates for both it and ''Way of the Jedi''.

==Plot summary==
{{Plot}}

==Continuity==
===Connections to other ''Star Wars'' works===
====''Star Wars: The Clone Wars''====
[[File:The_Clone_Wars_film_poster.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The plot of ''The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny: Tethan Battle Adventure'' closely ties in to that of the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film (poster pictured).]]
The events of ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' take place concurrently with<ref name="Tethan">''The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny: Tethan Battle Adventure''</ref> those of the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film.<ref name="TCWfilm">[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (film)|''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film]]</ref> They begin by depicting the arrival of the [[Galactic Republic/Legends|Galactic Republic]] [[Low Altitude Assault Transport/infantry/Legends|gunships]] on the [[Planet/Legends|planet]] [[Teth/Legends|Teth]] and, in some adventure paths, go on to feature the Republic [[All Terrain Tactical Enforcer/Legends|All Terrain Tactical Enforcer]] [[Walker/Legends|walkers]] scaling a cliff there to reach the [[B'omarr Monastery]] atop it,<ref name="Tethan" /> all of which is also seen in the film.<ref name="TCWfilm" /> The book includes the scene&mdash;with nearly identical dialogue<ref name="Tethan" />&mdash;from the film where [[Anakin Skywalker/Legends|Anakin Skywalker]] contacts [[Obi-Wan Kenobi/Legends|Obi-Wan Kenobi]] while [[Mission to Jabba's Palace (Clone Wars)/Legends|hurtling]] toward the surface of [[Tatooine/Legends|Tatooine]] in the [[G9 Rigger-class light freighter/Legends|freighter]] [[Twilight/Legends|''Twilight'']],<ref name="TCWfilm" /> although in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' the scene is presented from Kenobi's point of view. The book also features a portion of the film's [[Lightsaber duel/Legends|lightsaber duel]] between Kenobi and [[Asajj Ventress/Legends|Asajj Ventress]],<ref name="Tethan" /> albeit with some alterations,<ref name="TCWfilm" /> including the direct involvement of the book's [[Unidentified Tethan youngling|protagonist]] in the confrontation.<ref name="Tethan" />

''Tethan Battle Adventure'' includes a number of references to<ref name="Tethan" /> the Republic rescuing the kidnapped [[Hutt/Legends|Hutt]] [[Youngling/Legends|infant]] [[Rotta/Legends|Rotta]],<ref name="TCWfilm" /> [[Dooku/Legends|Dooku]] visiting Tatooine on his [[Count Dooku's solar sailer/Legends|personal starship]], and Ventress searching for Skywalker at the B'omarr Monastery<ref name="Tethan" />&mdash;all of which are also plot elements of the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film.<ref name="TCWfilm" /> The book features the [[Protocol droid/Legends|protocol droid]] [[TC-70/Legends|TC-70]],<ref name="Tethan" /> originally introduced in the film<ref name="TCWfilm" /> and its [[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (novelization)|novelization]],<ref name="TCWnovel">[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (novelization)|''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' novelization]]</ref> and similarly to the film,<ref name="TCWfilm" /> an adventure path in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' includes several [[Rogue-class Porax-38 starfighter/Legends|starfighters]] [[Pilot/Legends|piloted]] by [[IG-100 MagnaGuard/Legends|IG-100 MagnaGuard]] [[Droid/Legends|droids]] intercepting the [[Starship/Legends|starship]] on which the protagonist arrives in the [[Tatoo system/Legends|Tatoo system]]. Scenes [[Content cut from Star Wars|cut]] from the final release of the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film were also referenced in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' in the form of a [[Jungle rancor/Legends|rancor]] being encountered at the B'omarr Monastery.<ref name="Tethan" />

====Other media====
[[File:UltimateWeaponPlans-AOTC.png|thumb|left|250px|''Tethan Battle Adventure'' features an appearance of the schematics of the early incarnation of what would eventually become the Death Star (schematics of the pre-Death Star "Ultimate Weapon" pictured).]]
''Tethan Battle Adventure'' references other ''[[Star Wars]]'' films, as well. For example, it includes a use of<ref name="Tethan" /> the reoccurring phrase "[[I have a bad feeling about this]]."<ref name="TPM">{{Film|I}}</ref><ref name="AOTC">{{Film|II}}</ref><ref name="ROTS">{{Film|III}}</ref><ref name="ANH">{{Film|IV}}</ref><ref name="ESB">{{Film|V}}</ref><ref name="ROTJ">{{Film|VI}}</ref> It builds on<ref name="Tethan" /> [[Star Wars: Episode II Attack of the Clones|''Star Wars'': Episode II ''Attack of the Clones'']] establishing that the [[Death Star/Legends|superweapon]]<ref name="AOTC" /> that would become the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station/Legends|Death Star]]<ref name="ANH" /> was at one point developed by the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems/Legends|Confederacy of Independent Systems]].<ref name="AOTC" /> Furthermore, in some adventure paths Kenobi<ref name="Tethan" />&mdash;who, in [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars'': Episode IV ''A New Hope'']], is the first aboard the [[Millennium Falcon/Legends|''Millennium Falcon'']] to recognize the Death Star as a very large [[Battle station/Legends|space station]]<ref name="ANH" />&mdash;is made aware of the concept of the [[Superweapon/Legends|superweapon]]. There are narrative echoes of ''A New Hope'' elsewhere in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' as well, including a [[Unidentified Sullustan (Raidos)|patron]] of a [[Unidentified Raidos cantina|cantina]] rapidly becoming angered after encountering the inexperienced protagonist, the severing of a [[Limb/Legends|hand]] during a cantina fight that involves a [[Lightsaber/Legends|lightsaber]]-wielding [[Pon'Dart|combatant]], and the utterance of phrases "Aren't you a little small to be a clone trooper?" and "That explosion is nothing compared to the strength of the Force!",<ref name="Tethan" /> reminiscent of ''A New Hope''{{'s}} "Aren't you a little short for a stormtrooper?" and "The ability to destroy a planet is insignificant next to the power of the Force."<ref name="ANH" />

Like in [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']],<ref name="ROTJ" /> in an adventure path where the protagonist of ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' lands in a [[Rancor/Legends|rancor]] pit, they rush toward a door with a grille in it in order to escape, only to be blocked by the presence of hostile parties on the other side of it. The book also prominently features [[Janu Godalhi/Legends|Janu Godalhi]],<ref name="Tethan" /> who originated as a background character in [[Star Wars: Episode III Revenge of the Sith|''Star Wars'': Episode III ''Revenge of the Sith'']].<ref name="ROTS" /><ref name="DB Janu Godalhi">{{DB|character|janugodalhi|Godalhi, Janu}}</ref> The majority of the flesh of ''Revenge of the Sith''{{'s}} [[Cyborg/Legends|cyborg]] [[Grievous/Legends|Grievous]] was constituted by his [[Brain/Legends|brain]] and internal organs,<ref name="CVD2016">[[Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary (2016)|''Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary'', 2016 edition]]</ref> and in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'', in an adventure path where Dooku offers the protagonist to begin [[Sith training]], the former also threatens to use the latter's brain and [[Heart/Legends|heart]] to create "the next generation of cyborgs."<ref name="Tethan" />

[[File:Cloud_City.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A number of references in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' are connected to Cloud City (pictured) from ''Star Wars'': Episode V ''The Empire Strikes Back''.]]
Similarly to ''[[Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic]]'', which introduced the character [[Galduran Calrissian]],<ref name="KOTOR">''[[Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic]]''</ref> the namesake of [[Lando Calrissian/Legends|Lando Calrissian]] of [[Star Wars: Episode V The Empire Strikes Back|''Star Wars'': Episode V ''The Empire Strikes Back'']],<ref name="ESB" /> ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' features the new character [[Raan Calrissian]]. Both Raan<ref name="Tethan" /> and Lando are first encountered in [[Cloud City/Legends|Cloud City]],<ref name="ESB" /> and the line "What have we here?" is a part of both characters' opening dialogue.<ref name="Tethan" /><ref name="ESB" /> In addition, Raan pilots the starship ''[[Jostaar Express]]'' while delivering a shipment to a client on<ref name="Tethan" /> the [[Kibilini sector/Legends|Kibilini sector]]<ref name="EAOC Socorro">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-the-essential-atlas-online-companion|text=''Star Wars: The Essential Atlas'' Online Companion|int=Star Wars: The Essential Atlas Online Companion}} &mdash; Based on corresponding data for the [[Socorro system/Legends|Socorro system]]</ref> world<ref name="TEA">''[[The Essential Atlas]]''</ref> [[Socorro/Legends|Socorro]],<ref name="Tethan" /> with Lando being a [[Socorran]]<ref name="Vegas">{{SW|url=news/viva-space-vegas-the-history-of-the-marvelous-wheel-part-1|series=Viva Space Vegas! The History of the Marvelous Wheel|text=Part 1|nolive=1}}</ref> who was [[Reproduction/Legends|born]] in [[31 BBY/Legends|31 BBY]]<ref name-"LandoBirthYear">{{Blog|holocron|13/comments|Major Character Birth Years}} states that [[Lando Calrissian/Legends|Lando Calrissian]] was [[Reproduction/Legends|born]] thirty-one [[Standard year/Legends|years]] prior to the events of {{Film|IV}}, which corresponds to [[31 BBY/Legends|31 BBY]] per ''[[The New Essential Chronology]]''.</ref> and the [[Jostaar system/Legends|Jostaar system]] later being placed in the Kibilini [[Sector/Legends|sector]].<ref name="EAOC">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-the-essential-atlas-online-companion|text=''Star Wars: The Essential Atlas'' Online Companion|int=Star Wars: The Essential Atlas Online Companion}}</ref>

References to other ''Star Wars'' works in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' are not restricted to films only. [[Cambylictus berry|Camby berries]]<ref name="Tethan" /> were introduced in the ''[[Star Wars Galaxies]]'' [[video game]] in [[2008]],<ref name="Love">{{SonyCite|url=players/news_archive.vm?id=67596&month=022008|text=Friday Feature &ndash; Celebrate the Love}}</ref> while the "Rutanian Senatorial process"<ref name="Tethan" /> refers to the [[Rutanian]] [[Species/Legends|species]] from the planet [[Rutan/Legends|Rutan]], both of which were introduced in [[Jedi Apprentice: The Shattered Peace|''The Shattered Peace'']], a [[2000]] installment of [[Jude Watson]]'s ''[[Star Wars: Jedi Apprentice]]'' juvenile novel series.<ref name="Shattered">''[[Jedi Apprentice: The Shattered Peace]]''</ref> [[Crystalline vertex/Legends|Crystalline vertex]]<ref name="Tethan" /> was first mentioned in [[Brian Daley]]'s ''[[Han Solo at Stars' End]]'', the [[1979]] first entry of the novel series ''[[Star Wars: The Han Solo Adventures]]''.<ref name="StarsEnd">''[[Han Solo at Stars' End]]''</ref> Finally, the Cloud City adventure paths in ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' also contain allusions to two<ref name="Tethan" /> [[2002]]<ref name="Crossfire">''[[Boba Fett: Crossfire]]''</ref><ref name="TCWgame">[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (video game)|''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' video game]]</ref> works in the form of the character [[Glynn-Beti]] from [[Terry Bisson]]'s juvenile novel [[Boba Fett: Crossfire|''Crossfire'']] of the [[Star Wars: Boba Fett (novel series)|''Star Wars: Boba Fett'']] series<ref name="Crossfire" /> and the [[Clone Wars/Legends|Clone Wars]] [[Battle of Bespin (Clone Wars)|battle]] on [[Bespin/Legends|Bespin]], first mentioned in the [[Star Wars: The Clone Wars (video game)|''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' video game]].<ref name="TCWgame" />

===Asset reuse and contradictions===
[[File:CloneTrooperSquadron-TethanBattleAdventure.png|thumb|left|250px|A scene from the online content accompanying ''Tethan Battle Adventure'']]
The accompanying [[StarWars.com]] online content made use of several models<ref name="Tethan" /> from the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film. The model of [[Clone Captain/Legends|Clone Captain]] [[Rex/Legends|Rex]]<ref name="TCWfilm" /> was used to represent both another, unnamed [[Unidentified Clone Captain (Teth)|Clone Captain]] as well as the [[Unidentified clone trooper leader|leader]] of a "[[Squadron/Legends|squadron]]" of [[Clone trooper/Legends|clone troopers]],<ref name="Tethan" /> while the models of [[Clone Marshal Commander/Legends|clone commander]] [[Cody/Legends|Cody]] and<ref name="TCWfilm" /> a [[Coruscant Guard/Legends|Coruscant Guard]] [[Clone shock trooper/Legends|clone shock trooper]]<ref name="SWEfox">{{SWE|characters|clone-commander-fox|Clone Commander Fox}}</ref> stood in for generic clone troopers during the [[Battle of Teth/Legends|Battle of Teth]].<ref name="Tethan" /> The likeness of the Confederacy of Independent Systems [[General/Legends|general]] [[Whorm Loathsom/Legends|Whorm Loathsom]]<ref name="TCWfilm" /> was also used to depict a generic patron of a [[Cantina/Legends|cantina]] in the [[City/Legends|city]] of [[Raidos]] on Teth. Even though Anakin Skywalker himself appeared in the online content, his obscured character model was also used to represent Janu Godalhi. Elsewhere, Godalhi was instead depicted via a model of a [[Cloning/Legends|clone]] [[Trooper/Legends|trooper]] whereas Skywalker's likeness stood in for the book's protagonist. In addition, a screenshot<ref name="Tethan" /> from a deleted scene of the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film featuring a [[Lightsaber combat/Legends|lightsaber duel]] between Skywalker and Ventress<ref name="TCWfilm" /> was used to represent a [[Force vision/Legends|Force vision]] in which the protagonist has to choose between following either of the two [[Force-sensitive/Legends|Force-users]].<ref name="Tethan" />

[[File:TatooineMap-TheWorldOfSWKenobi.png|thumb|right|250px|''Tethan Battle Adventure'' misidentifies the Northern Dune Sea as the Western Dune Sea (Tatooine map depicting both regions pictured).]]
''Tethan Battle Adventure'' contains an apparent internal contradiction where, in an adventure path the reader may take in the book's story, Obi-Wan Kenobi takes a [[Transport/Legends|transport]] back to the ''[[Spirit of the Republic]]'' in order to travel from Teth to Tatooine, claiming that his "fighter was badly damaged" upon his arrival on the former world. In another path, however, Kenobi uses his [[Obi-Wan Kenobi's Delta-7B Aethersprite-class light interceptor/Legends|starfighter]] to travel to the ''Spirit of the Republic''.<ref name="Tethan" /> In the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' film, while Kenobi's [[Delta-7B Aethersprite-class light interceptor/Legends|fighter]] is shown as participating in the Battle of Teth, it is not depicted as suffering any serious damage.<ref name="TCWfilm" /> ''Tethan Battle Adventure'' also misidentifies<ref name="Tethan" /> the [[Northern Dune Sea/Legends|Northern Dune Sea]] region of Tatooine, which was located adjacent to [[Jabba's Palace/Legends|Jabba's Palace]],<ref name="TatooineMap">{{SW|url=news/the-world-of-star-wars-kenobi|text=The World of ''Star Wars: Kenobi''|nolive=1}}</ref> as the [[Western Dune Sea/Legends|Western Dune Sea]],<ref name="Tethan" /> which was situated considerably further away from the palace.<ref name="TatooineMap" />

